# {{ options.artifact_name }}

Generated backend: Java 21, Spring Boot 4.1.2, Gradle 9.5.1,
JPA/Hibernate, Jakarta Validation, Jackson, springdoc 3.2.0 and PostgreSQL.

Includes entities, repositories, validated DTOs, transactional services and REST
controllers. Single-column references and 1:1 / 1:N relationships use scalar
reference IDs and database constraints, with navigation endpoints. Composite IDs,
N:M join tables, inheritance and relationships requiring additional runtime
rules (including composition) are rejected, never silently omitted.
No UML operation is converted into invented business logic. Read-only entities
omit all mutation endpoints. Auditing and custom business methods are not generated.

## Build

Install Java 21 and Gradle 9.5.1, then run `gradle build`.
Optionally create the standard wrapper using `gradle wrapper --gradle-version 9.5.1`.
No database or credentials are required to compile. Dependencies require network
access on the first build. No wrapper binaries are included in this increment.

## Run against a dedicated PostgreSQL database

Create an empty database and apply `schema.sql` using psql with
`ON_ERROR_STOP=1`. This script intentionally fails on existing tables rather
than overwriting or migrating data. Supply `DB_URL` (JDBC PostgreSQL URL),
`DB_USER` and `DB_PASSWORD` through the environment, then run `gradle bootRun`.
The application validates the schema instead of modifying it at startup.
Do not use the legacy CASE database as the target.

The generated foundation has no application authentication or authorization;
CASE project permissions are not a security policy for generated business data.
Keep it in a controlled development environment until a policy is specified.

